        "id": 3513462
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-09T11:18:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As far as I know, the FMAP and CBI are more like \"data\" stored in write-protected region; When the ec needs to access them, it goes through `flash_read` instead of accessing them directly from SRAM. \nIn this case, do we still need to copy them into SRAM during sysjump?",
      "parentUuid": "e0776c7f_83779e30",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 29,
        "startChar": 15,
        "endLine": 29,
        "endChar": 22
      },
      "revId": "0be955d5956e7184cbdab60acc9a47653d0c0a92",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c3a981a6_93db6c53",
        "filename": "zephyr/boards/google/realtek/realtek_rts5912.dts",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 29,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-09T22:45:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You\u0027re correct that CBI is accessed through a flash read operation, so it can be located outside the ec-ro section.\n\nThe fmap section isn\u0027t currently accessed by the runtime EC firmware, so it doesn\u0027t have to be copied for correct operation.\n\nOther EC architectures have included this in the area that is copied to SRAM, but it looks to be safe to skip copying FMAP to SRAM.",
      "parentUuid": "c21d4077_5c3fa4f8",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 29,
        "startChar": 15,
        "endLine": 29,
        "endChar": 22
      },
      "revId": "0be955d5956e7184cbdab60acc9a47653d0c0a92",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
